1
00:00:00,577 --> 00:00:03,079
[SPEAKER_01]: I have one sutta here and a few questions.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:12,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Is paying attention to pleasant breathing sensations a valid practice for developing of the right view?

3
00:00:14,146 --> 00:00:14,326
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

4
00:00:15,047 --> 00:00:15,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely not.

5
00:00:22,372 --> 00:00:29,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And it seems that that kind of practice, to pay attention to pleasant breathing sensations,

6
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:34,241
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems to be just another sensual act.

7
00:00:35,741 --> 00:00:50,066
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it's not the same as a sexual act, but it's still a pleasant physical act that one can absorb into and therefore try to escape suffering through pleasure, which is the point of any sensual act.

8
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:52,087
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, it is.

9
00:00:53,147 --> 00:00:56,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Any sensations, basically, is just another word for tactile.

10
00:00:57,669 --> 00:01:00,570
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it means it's a tactile sense,

11
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:13,558
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes on the base of your body, and even if it's not necessarily pleasant, if your practice is just to attend to the sensations of the tactile nature, which is the nature of sensations, there are no sensations that are not tactile.

12
00:01:14,398 --> 00:01:20,422
[SPEAKER_01]: People often tend to assume sensations are like vedanā and feelings, but they're not.

13
00:01:21,543 --> 00:01:30,148
[SPEAKER_01]: They're completely tactile, like sensation of of things touching your skin, or sensations on the inside of your body that you can have a tactile sense of.

14
00:01:31,331 --> 00:01:34,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, observing sensations, it's always going to be on the level of perception.

15
00:01:35,392 --> 00:01:37,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sensations can never be feelings.

16
00:01:38,173 --> 00:01:40,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as the suttas say, what is feeling?

17
00:01:40,233 --> 00:01:41,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, feeling is that which feels.

18
00:01:42,234 --> 00:01:42,994
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is perception?

19
00:01:43,034 --> 00:01:44,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception is that which perceives.

20
00:01:45,275 --> 00:01:47,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So, perception perceives, feeling feels.

21
00:01:48,275 --> 00:01:53,717
[SPEAKER_01]: But people kind of, through that kind of muddleness of their own understanding and information,

22
00:01:54,928 --> 00:02:07,990
[SPEAKER_01]: While they're perceiving a mere tactile sensations, they think they're being mindful of vedanā, of feeling, which is always completely the opposite direction of where your attention is.

23
00:02:08,190 --> 00:02:20,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in expecting that the right view would come on a kind of close watching of tactile sensations, you can see how mistaken that is on so many levels.

24
00:02:21,032 --> 00:02:24,513
[SPEAKER_01]: It implies the truth comes from just magical appearance through your senses,

25
00:02:25,497 --> 00:02:30,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, beyond your senses, and it presents itself through some practice of observation.

26
00:02:31,982 --> 00:02:33,083
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not understanding.

27
00:02:33,944 --> 00:02:41,390
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just witnessing a random thing, that then you believe to be your enlightenment, or sotāpārtha, or whatever else.

28
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:45,333
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you read the sutta, there's just no mention of that whatsoever.

29
00:02:46,334 --> 00:02:50,197
[SPEAKER_01]: It starts with the gradual training, withdrawal from unwholesome, and then discerning

30
00:02:52,692 --> 00:02:55,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Skillful is skillful and unskillful is unskillful.

31
00:02:55,353 --> 00:03:03,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing the root of the unwholesome, discerning it, fully understanding it, not not feeding it through your ignorant actions and wrong attention.

32
00:03:04,297 --> 00:03:06,638
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you undo the entire saṃsāra.

33
00:03:07,778 --> 00:03:14,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Through the diligent work of abstaining from things that should be abstained and cultivating things that should be cultivated.

34
00:03:15,002 --> 00:03:20,464
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could see how the modern practice of sensation watching, as I often said, it's like like staring at a

35
00:03:23,792 --> 00:03:32,046
[SPEAKER_01]: As I often said, it's like staring at the, basically, trying to hunt down the moment where the truth will just magically reveal itself.

36
00:03:32,086 --> 00:03:35,271
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could see how that idea would be popular.

37
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:47,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it kind of does away with all the tedious part of pain and development that you have to go through, as with anything new that you're learning and picking up a craft and a skill and understanding.

38
00:03:48,346 --> 00:04:01,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all I need to do is just get closer and closer and closer to my sensation watching, to observing the tactile things on the basis of my body, and then the truth will just hit me, and I'll just gonna have a magical experience.

39
00:04:02,132 --> 00:04:04,053
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, from then onwards, everything will be fine.

40
00:04:05,430 --> 00:04:11,214
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, it's completely circumstantial, random, and as I said, it does not exist in the suttas at all.

41
00:04:13,236 --> 00:04:15,338
[SPEAKER_00]: At least not in the early suttas that we talked about.

42
00:04:16,659 --> 00:04:20,542
[SPEAKER_01]: The the later developments of Buddhism, they might have developed their own scripture to support this.

43
00:04:20,862 --> 00:04:29,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Certainly commentaries invented this whole thing, starting from Buddha Gosa and Visuddhimāga, and Abhidhamma before that, but that's not found in the suttas.

44
00:04:29,649 --> 00:04:32,771
[SPEAKER_01]: The path of purification, how they call it, was very clear.

45
00:04:32,831 --> 00:04:35,013
[SPEAKER_01]: The gradual training, and he said, like,

46
00:04:36,487 --> 00:04:53,474
[SPEAKER_01]: If anybody, the Buddha said, if anybody, even in the past, would have attained liberation, or anything approximating liberation, they would have had to do it only through this way, of purifying virtue, your actions, and full understanding of the the the the intentions, which are the root of karma, basically.

47
00:04:54,715 --> 00:05:00,757
[SPEAKER_01]: And if somebody claims that they can develop full enlightenment, purification, apart from this, such thing is not possible.

48
00:05:01,798 --> 00:05:04,699
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you ask yourself, like, when you're watching your sensations,

49
00:05:06,013 --> 00:05:07,394
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you implying?

50
00:05:08,214 --> 00:05:09,715
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you emphasizing?

51
00:05:10,235 --> 00:05:11,556
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you expect to happen?

52
00:05:14,717 --> 00:05:17,179
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you expect to occur?

53
00:05:18,659 --> 00:05:29,185
[SPEAKER_01]: And then see whether it aligns with these ideas that you can broadly find in the suttas without any kind of great effort of insight and understanding.

54
00:05:29,805 --> 00:05:35,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Just through being informed with the suttas, you get the general sense of what practice kind of boils down to,

55
00:05:36,562 --> 00:05:38,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And then see, is that what you're expecting?

56
00:05:38,283 --> 00:05:39,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that what you're doing?

57
00:05:39,104 --> 00:05:45,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that what you're expecting to happen when you're watching your sensations and committed to, like, concentrating harder and harder and harder?

58
00:05:47,130 --> 00:06:00,740
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I often quoted that sutta, which was actually two suttas, that I kind of joined into one, where the Buddha compared any such meditation to sensuality.

59
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:02,702
[SPEAKER_01]: It says premeditate, mismeditate,

60
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:05,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Over-meditate, under-meditate.

61
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:23,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, when you're wrongly attending to your objects, if you're expecting your meditation, your samādhi, to come out of focusing on an object, it's already a form of meditation with sensuality, because you're emphasizing the object of your senses, tactile.

62
00:06:23,985 --> 00:06:32,827
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, in another sutta, the Mara uses the same wording of pre-meditate, mis-meditate, over-meditate, and then he adds this similes to illustrate that.

63
00:06:33,667 --> 00:06:43,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying, oh, it's like a jackal waiting down, hunting down a mouse, waiting for it to appear to pounce on, or an owl, a fish, and things like that.

64
00:06:43,874 --> 00:06:51,039
[SPEAKER_01]: You are expecting a novelty experience to come, and then, when you get it, then that's a success.

65
00:06:51,219 --> 00:06:51,759
[SPEAKER_01]: You caught it.

66
00:06:51,979 --> 00:06:52,720
[SPEAKER_01]: You hunted it down.

67
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:53,360
[SPEAKER_01]: You got it now.

68
00:06:54,121 --> 00:06:59,164
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, that direction... and I'm not denying the reality of that, by the way.

69
00:07:00,656 --> 00:07:02,136
[SPEAKER_01]: The reality of novelty experiences.

70
00:07:02,296 --> 00:07:03,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you can get loads.

71
00:07:03,817 --> 00:07:07,417
[SPEAKER_01]: What I'm saying is, the dhamma practice is completely the opposite direction from that.

72
00:07:08,497 --> 00:07:24,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where your attention is, when you are focusing on your tactile sensations, watching moment to moment, moment to moment, expecting kalāpas to reveal the truth to you, and so on, the dhamma, the understanding, would be completely at the back of that, where you are not looking.

73
00:07:25,740 --> 00:07:29,421
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would you, would you say that that mean, what you mean there is,

74
00:07:30,951 --> 00:07:33,392
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be able to understand the nature of experience.

75
00:07:34,112 --> 00:07:43,795
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be able to understand the nature of your intentions, which begins through purifying your bodily behavior, verbal behavior and mental behavior.

76
00:07:44,995 --> 00:07:54,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are responsible for your thoughts, which ones you welcome, which ones you cultivate, which ones you allow to sort of linger around and which ones you ignore.

77
00:07:55,251 --> 00:07:56,452
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people don't even see that.

78
00:07:56,973 --> 00:08:10,068
[SPEAKER_01]: They might be able to take on the precepts, keep the keep the physical restraint, maybe police their speech a bit better and so on, but mentally, they're just subjected to what their mind thinks, and then they just try to get rid of it, get rid of these thoughts.

79
00:08:10,528 --> 00:08:14,433
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, they don't see that they are actually responsible for them.

80
00:08:16,415 --> 00:08:39,453
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the gradual training, that then, once your behavior and virtue has been established, you get to notice kind of the same pattern, if you're looking at things rightly, you start to notice the same pattern regarding your thoughts, which is the pattern of, oh, so there is a thought arisen, I haven't created it, the possibility presented itself to do this or to not do that.

81
00:08:40,534 --> 00:08:41,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one do I relish?

82
00:08:41,595 --> 00:08:42,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one do I emphasize?

83
00:08:42,715 --> 00:08:43,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one do I lean towards?

84
00:08:43,996 --> 00:08:44,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one do I cultivate?

85
00:08:45,017 --> 00:08:45,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one do I welcome?

86
00:08:47,162 --> 00:08:48,203
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I remove the welcoming.

87
00:08:48,643 --> 00:08:49,804
[SPEAKER_01]: I say, no, I should not welcome that.

88
00:08:50,285 --> 00:08:51,586
[SPEAKER_01]: But I don't try to get rid of it.

89
00:08:51,886 --> 00:08:52,867
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't try to deny it.

90
00:08:53,347 --> 00:08:55,549
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't try to focus away and pretend it's not there.

91
00:08:56,529 --> 00:09:06,237
[SPEAKER_01]: I basically take responsibility for the pressure that that thought exerts on me, without trying to destroy the thought, because that would be already overstepping into Māra's domain.

92
00:09:10,541 --> 00:09:13,863
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I just go to another question,

93
00:09:17,267 --> 00:09:24,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I not use such a practice of focusing on pleasant breathing to replace my coarse sensual acts?

94
00:09:25,749 --> 00:09:34,550
[SPEAKER_00]: If I find joy in simple attention to the breath, I hope that my mind will want that pleasure instead of other dangerous pleasures.

95
00:09:34,650 --> 00:09:40,532
[SPEAKER_00]: I hope to find a pleasure which is separate from sensuality, so that I can experience the joy of jhāna.

96
00:09:41,552 --> 00:09:43,832
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's, that's the... Yeah, that's the idea.

97
00:09:43,932 --> 00:09:45,093
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the basic idea, this.

98
00:09:45,473 --> 00:09:46,813
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the problem with that idea is,

99
00:09:47,766 --> 00:09:55,612
[SPEAKER_01]: That person, whoever asked that, they admit that focusing on the sensual pleasure of the breath is better than coarse sensuality.

100
00:09:56,333 --> 00:10:07,802
[SPEAKER_01]: But what they don't understand is that the danger of coarse sensuality is still present in the danger of your sensual breath.

101
00:10:07,902 --> 00:10:12,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the danger of sensual desire is not because they're coarse or subtle.

102
00:10:12,746 --> 00:10:14,428
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because they pertain to the body.

103
00:10:14,968 --> 00:10:16,950
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because they're tactile, because they're physical.

104
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:19,401
[SPEAKER_01]: The senses are Māra's domain.

105
00:10:20,061 --> 00:10:37,008
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fundamentally, what you would be doing in such practice is replacing one thing within Māra's domain with a subtler thing within Māra's domain, and thinking you're avoiding the danger of the Māra's domain, while still remaining very much within the same domain, unchanged.

106
00:10:38,609 --> 00:10:40,990
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why in that Anguttaraṅkāya...

107
00:10:41,550 --> 00:10:41,950
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't remember.

108
00:10:41,970 --> 00:10:43,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe we can quote it.

109
00:10:43,351 --> 00:10:45,192
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't remember the numbers.

110
00:10:45,432 --> 00:10:47,213
[SPEAKER_01]: It was basically what the Buddha said, that

111
00:10:49,047 --> 00:11:03,052
[SPEAKER_01]: That one could enter and abide, develop the first jhāna without having already fully seen the gratification, danger and escape in regard to sensuality and the five hindrances in general, such thing is not possible.

112
00:11:04,533 --> 00:11:09,855
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first you need to see that danger pertaining to anything that you would be attending for pleasure.

113
00:11:10,889 --> 00:11:12,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And completely abandon that attitude.

114
00:11:13,150 --> 00:11:19,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, and only then, the pleasure of jhāna can arise, when you don't look for it, don't need it, don't seek it.

115
00:11:19,993 --> 00:11:32,179
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why that pleasure of jhāna, that's not being sought in that sensual manner, is the pleasure that's not underlined by the underlying tendencies, as the suttas would say.

116
00:11:32,659 --> 00:11:33,760
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in Mahārāja's domain.

117
00:11:49,094 --> 00:12:00,123
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, from these questions, you know, it seems like you're suffering, you want to get rid of, well, free from suffering, get rid of suffering, you want to get right view.

118
00:12:02,304 --> 00:12:15,815
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of doing all these coarse, unwholesome actions, sexual actions, you, you go into these subtle actions of, you know, doesn't hurt anyone in, you know, in a coarse manner.

119
00:12:16,476 --> 00:12:17,977
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be the wrong view and so on.

120
00:12:21,677 --> 00:12:22,578
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still the same.

121
00:12:23,358 --> 00:12:24,218
[SPEAKER_00]: I want pleasure.

122
00:12:25,419 --> 00:12:26,099
[SPEAKER_00]: To get pleasure.

123
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:26,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

124
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:27,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm suffering.

125
00:12:28,081 --> 00:12:28,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

126
00:12:29,021 --> 00:12:30,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And pleasure is the freedom.

127
00:12:31,522 --> 00:12:36,325
[SPEAKER_01]: It's gonna be... it's inevitable that a person will be doing it to some extent.

128
00:12:37,186 --> 00:12:48,472
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, if you are withdrawn from unwholesome things, the coarse unwholesome things, say you're celibate, keeping the precepts and so on, you will be finding, you know, sensual, semi-sensual joy in other things.

129
00:12:49,503 --> 00:12:57,687
[SPEAKER_01]: On your day-to-day basis, like going for a walk, doing this, doing that, it's not always... but it could be sensual, in that sense.

130
00:12:57,907 --> 00:13:00,448
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that's not the main issue.

131
00:13:01,548 --> 00:13:12,393
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... if you continue with the purification of behavior, if you don't revert back to the coarse, unwholesome, clearly unwholesome pleasures, this will kind of level itself out.

132
00:13:13,196 --> 00:13:29,895
[SPEAKER_01]: The issue is, if you were to think that, for example, the pleasure of a nice walk in the nature, peaceful, beautiful sounds, birds chirping, if you were to hold the view that that is your jhāna, is the practice of dhamma, is getting rid of the sensuality.

133
00:13:30,556 --> 00:13:31,597
[SPEAKER_01]: That's really the problem.

134
00:13:31,857 --> 00:13:33,699
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, if a person is watching their breath,

135
00:13:34,420 --> 00:13:48,346
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said many times, to calm down because they were agitated or whatever else, but without implying with their view, with their emphasis, that this is the practice of samādhi and jhāna and right view, there would be no problem, really.

136
00:13:50,115 --> 00:13:51,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this will lead me to...

137
00:13:51,795 --> 00:13:52,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

138
00:13:52,216 --> 00:14:08,040
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the implication behind that action, which is that, oh, this is the practice that takes me away from sensuality, which it doesn't, into into the right view, that will then magically happen through the novelty pleasure that will arise on account of this refined sensuality.

139
00:14:08,060 --> 00:14:09,460
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, oh, that's the problem.

140
00:14:09,560 --> 00:14:10,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your view there.

141
00:14:11,480 --> 00:14:16,902
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't have that view, and then you watch your breath because you're bored or something, fine.

142
00:14:17,749 --> 00:14:20,292
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not gonna take you too far out.

143
00:14:20,512 --> 00:14:22,073
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not gonna sort of corrupt you.

144
00:14:23,935 --> 00:14:29,200
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize most often, more often than not, when people ask these questions, they don't ask them mutually.

145
00:14:29,380 --> 00:14:30,522
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't ask them in this sense.

146
00:14:30,602 --> 00:14:37,288
[SPEAKER_01]: They ask them is this a valid practice for enlightenment, for uprooting of sensuality, for becoming anāgāmī.

147
00:14:38,369 --> 00:14:40,091
[SPEAKER_01]: And as I said, it's a categorical no.

